00:12:03.200 --> 00:12:03.805
Okay now.

00:12:03.805 --> 00:12:06.340
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Let's go back then.

00:12:06.340 --> 00:12:11.120
So what is this strange title,
The Analysis of Algorithms?

00:12:11.120 --> 00:12:14.580
Well I can explain that, but
actually it goes back to a story.

00:12:16.760 --> 00:12:23.100
A couple years ago I was at a conference
of mathematicians in Santa Barbara.

00:12:23.100 --> 00:12:27.150
And somebody asked me at dinner,
what do you do?

00:12:27.150 --> 00:12:31.570
And I said I'm a computer scientist.

00:12:31.570 --> 00:12:33.430
And computer scientist.

00:12:33.430 --> 00:12:37.560
Are you a numerical analyst?

00:12:37.560 --> 00:12:41.970
And I said well, no, I like numbers and

00:12:41.970 --> 00:12:46.570
things like this but numerical analysis
isn't quite the description of what I do.

00:12:46.570 --> 00:12:50.040
So, I said, artificial intelligence?

00:12:50.040 --> 00:12:52.620
And, I said, well, no,
not really that either.

00:12:52.620 --> 00:12:57.990
And, so, the guy says,
then you must be a language man.

00:12:57.990 --> 00:13:04.300
You see, in those days, computer science
consisted of three possible things.

00:13:04.300 --> 00:13:05.659
One was numerical analysis.

00:13:06.952 --> 00:13:12.790
One was artificial intelligence and
third was programming languages.

00:13:12.790 --> 00:13:21.070
Study of languages, formal languages
that are used to describe algorithms.

00:13:21.070 --> 00:13:21.570
Okay, well.

00:13:23.020 --> 00:13:26.920
But it occurred to me that really I
wasn't in any of those categories and so

00:13:26.920 --> 00:13:30.330
I had no way to explain what I do,
or what I did.

00:13:30.330 --> 00:13:33.910
And so that night I started
thinking about it and said,

00:13:33.910 --> 00:13:37.540
well wait a minute,
I gotta have a name for what I like to do.

00:13:40.170 --> 00:13:44.970
And so I came up with the,
to say I'm okay,

00:13:44.970 --> 00:13:49.350
the next time somebody asks me what I do,
I'm gonna say an analysis of algorithms.

00:13:49.350 --> 00:13:51.910
I didn't really know exactly
what it meant at the time but

00:13:51.910 --> 00:13:54.240
I knew that I needed names so
I pictured it, you know,

00:13:54.240 --> 00:13:59.470
Richard Bellman made up the name
dynamic programming and everybody gots

00:13:59.470 --> 00:14:04.070
started using it and so I figured if I
started talking about it long enough,

00:14:04.070 --> 00:14:10.520
it would become a well known display.

00:14:10.520 --> 00:14:17.430
But anyway, I did know that what I
liked to do was analyze algorithms.

00:14:17.430 --> 00:14:20.100
I mean I liked algorithms.

00:14:20.100 --> 00:14:22.564
I don't have to explain to you
guys what an algorithm is.

00:14:22.564 --> 00:14:31.590
But when I went to quantitatively studying
algorithms and not just qualitatively.

00:14:31.590 --> 00:14:35.320
So I'm gonna explain to you by example.

00:14:35.320 --> 00:14:38.550
I still haven't really come up
with a perfect definition of what

00:14:38.550 --> 00:14:40.930
it means The Analysis of Algorithms.

00:14:40.930 --> 00:14:45.110
But by example,
I mean the working definition is,

00:14:46.390 --> 00:14:49.485
whatever I'm interested
in is analysis of value.

00:14:49.485 --> 00:14:53.470
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I tend to refine this over the years,

00:14:53.470 --> 00:15:00.240
but by giving an example,
I think I can explain to you what is.

00:15:00.240 --> 00:15:03.640
&gt;&gt; What is sort of special about it.

00:15:03.640 --> 00:15:05.040
So, let's take an example.

00:15:05.040 --> 00:15:10.750
I want to,
I'm gonna work a simple algorithm

00:15:12.920 --> 00:15:20.780
so that we can sort of analyze
it to the hilt and here we go.

00:15:20.780 --> 00:15:24.910
So the algorithm is going to be defined as

00:15:24.910 --> 00:15:30.104
the maximum of n number.

00:15:30.104 --> 00:15:36.370
So one through xn,
got to find the maximum.

00:15:38.150 --> 00:15:40.010
So here we go.

00:15:40.010 --> 00:15:40.580
Start out.

00:15:41.810 --> 00:15:46.866
&gt;&gt; Come in here and the first step is

00:15:46.866 --> 00:15:52.684
I set m to x sub n, and I set k to n- 1.

00:15:56.304 --> 00:16:00.724
All right, that's the very first step.

00:16:00.724 --> 00:16:07.287
After that I make a test.

00:16:07.287 --> 00:16:13.234
Is K=0 or not, okay.

00:16:13.234 --> 00:16:18.400
Sorry, I haven't developed
my skills at writing good

00:16:18.400 --> 00:16:22.140
zeroes on the blackboard here.

00:16:24.629 --> 00:16:29.690
So I got two variables here, M and K,

00:16:29.690 --> 00:16:34.588
and I start out and I, that way, and

00:16:34.588 --> 00:16:39.670
so it might be that K is equal to zero.

00:16:41.000 --> 00:16:43.770
And then I'm gonna come do that,
but if K is not zero.

00:16:45.370 --> 00:16:51.190
Then I've got to make another test and
I will write that down so

00:16:51.190 --> 00:16:57.965
that is Xk bigger than M and
again, it's yes or no.

00:17:00.395 --> 00:17:03.870
So, I'm gonna write, I'm to put this
logarithm down, and we'll play it,

00:17:03.870 --> 00:17:04.525
through it.

00:17:04.525 --> 00:17:06.463
And, you'll understand
exactly what it does.

00:17:10.603 --> 00:17:17.272
So, the S is saying, m equates by x k,

00:17:17.272 --> 00:17:22.908
and no says k is set to k minus 1.

00:17:22.908 --> 00:17:28.410
And these come out here and
go all the way back.

00:17:28.410 --> 00:17:32.225
And so I got the algorithm out here
except for what happens under yes,

00:17:32.225 --> 00:17:36.995
and at the end here it says output m.

00:17:38.895 --> 00:17:40.295
And there we are.

00:17:40.295 --> 00:17:42.545
Okay, so this is, I think,

00:17:42.545 --> 00:17:47.625
if anybody here has written this half
a dozen programs they probably came

00:17:47.625 --> 00:17:53.390
up with a loop somewhere in one of those
programs that's very much like, like this.

00:17:53.390 --> 00:17:57.380
So it should be fairly familiar.

00:17:57.380 --> 00:18:00.930
But let's take an example and
play through.

00:18:00.930 --> 00:18:06.087
So suppose that n is 4 and

00:18:06.087 --> 00:18:11.000
the x1 is say 2.3 and

00:18:11.000 --> 00:18:17.398
x2 is 7.1, x3 is 4.0.

00:18:17.398 --> 00:18:22.748
X4 is 5.9, by the way if
anybody has a question on what

00:18:22.748 --> 00:18:27.765
I'm saying stop me right
away.x I want to know unless

00:18:27.765 --> 00:18:34.007
you don't understand anything and
then you can't ask anything.

00:18:34.007 --> 00:18:37.463
&gt;&gt; [LAUGHTER] But anyway, ask me and

00:18:37.463 --> 00:18:42.490
I'll try to answer
[INAUDIBLE] your chance okay?

00:18:42.490 --> 00:18:46.820
So, we start out and
the first part of, and

00:18:46.820 --> 00:18:51.080
we set m is going to be,

00:18:51.080 --> 00:18:57.290
is going to be set to 5.9 which is x4, xn?

00:18:57.290 --> 00:19:02.180
Is 5.9 and k is set to n-1 which is 3.

00:19:02.180 --> 00:19:04.850
All right, so that's this step.

00:19:04.850 --> 00:19:07.840
We come down here and we say is k 0?

00:19:07.840 --> 00:19:08.340
Nope.

00:19:09.710 --> 00:19:13.430
So then we check if xk is greater than m.

00:19:13.430 --> 00:19:20.862
So xk is 4.0, is 4.0 bigger than 5.9,
nope, it's not.

00:19:20.862 --> 00:19:24.170
So, we down here we change K to 2 and
go back,

00:19:29.290 --> 00:19:34.310
now again we ask is K through 0 it's not,
so we come through and

00:19:34.310 --> 00:19:36.310
now we check X equals.

00:19:36.310 --> 00:19:39.900
7.1 X2 against M.

